issues.body:
When carry binded on any mouse button, block pick doesnt work. Mod blocking this ability like im carrying some mob and return hotbar active to current slot.

Steps to reproduce the behavior:
1. Bind Pick up block/entity to mouse.4 (or mouse.5).
2. In-world, press the middle mouse button to pick-block a block that is already in another hotbar slot.
3. Carry On registers a phantom pickup → the player enters the "carrying" state → InventoryMixin cancels Inventory#pickSlot / setPickedItem / swapPaint → pick-block no longer works and the hotbar selection snaps back.

- 1.21.1
- NeoForge 21.1.248
- Carry On 2.2.6

I personally fixed it with vibe-coded follow-mod. Explanation by claude code:

Binding the same action to a keyboard key works fine, because the keyboard path uses carryKey.isDown(), while mouse binds go through the broken checkMouse().

Root cause

CarryOnCommonClient.checkMouse() (decompiled):

private static boolean checkMouse() {
    Minecraft mc = Minecraft.getInstance();
    if (carryKey.matchesMouse(0) && mc.mouseHandler.isLeftPressed())   return true; // 0 = left   ✓
    if (carryKey.matchesMouse(1) && mc.mouseHandler.isRightPressed())  return true; // 1 = right  ✓
    if (carryKey.matchesMouse(3) && mc.mouseHandler.isMiddlePressed()) return true; // ✗ mismatch
    return false;
}

Mouse button values (InputConstants / GLFW) are: left = 0, right = 1, middle = 2, mouse.4 = 3, mouse.5 = 4.

The third check asks matchesMouse(3) — i.e. "is the key bound to mouse.4?" — but then reads mouseHandler.isMiddlePressed(), i.e. "is the physical middle button (2) down?". So:

Bind on mouse.4 (value 3) → triggers whenever the middle button is pressed (which is vanilla pick-block). ← the reported break
Bind on the real middle button (value 2) → never checked (matchesMouse(2) is missing) → never fires.
Bind on mouse.5 (value 4) → never checked → never fires.

Net effect: no mouse button binds correctly to its own button; one of them actively hijacks pick-block.

Suggested fix

Map the bound mouse button to its own pressed state instead of hard-coding pairs. Read the actual bound button and query that exact button (e.g. via GLFW), which covers left/right/middle and side buttons uniformly:

private static boolean checkMouse() {
    Minecraft mc = Minecraft.getInstance();
    long window = mc.getWindow().getWindow();
    for (int button = 0; button <= 7; button++) {           // GLFW mouse buttons 0..7
        if (carryKey.matchesMouse(button)) {
            return GLFW.glfwGetMouseButton(window, button) == GLFW.GLFW_PRESS;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

This makes every mouse-button bind trigger on the button it's actually assigned to, and stops mouse.4/mouse.5 binds from colliding with middle-click pick-block.

(Verified locally: a client-side mixin that replaces checkMouse() with the loop above resolves the issue while keeping keyboard binds unchanged.)

